# PAM service for `lemondx serve --auth pam`, i.e. the stack looked up as
# --pam-service (default: lemondx). Install it as root:
#
#   install -m 644 systemd/lemondx.pam /etc/pam.d/lemondx
#
# Without it PAM falls back to its `other` stack, which on most distributions
# is pam_deny and refuses every login with no explanation -- `serve` warns at
# startup when /etc/pam.d/<service> is missing for that reason.
#
# This file is written for Debian/Ubuntu, whose system stack lives in
# common-auth/common-account. On Fedora/RHEL/Arch (and anything else with a
# single system-auth) comment the two @include lines out and uncomment the
# pair below them instead -- there is no spelling that works on both.
#
# Delegating to the host's own stack is the point: whatever the distribution
# already does for console and SSH logins -- pam_unix, faillock, SSSD, an MFA
# module -- applies here too, and stays applied when the host's policy changes.

@include common-auth
@include common-account

# auth     include  system-auth
# account  include  system-auth

# lemondx never opens a session or changes a password: pam.py calls only
# pam_authenticate() and pam_acct_mgmt(). These two make that explicit, so a
# future caller gets a clear refusal rather than silently picking up `other`.
password required pam_deny.so
session  required pam_deny.so

# Who may log in is decided by lemondx, not here: --pam-admin-group (default
# lxd / incus-admin) and --pam-read-group map group membership to a role, and
# anyone in neither is refused even with a correct password. Adding a
# pam_succeed_if here would only duplicate that in a second place that the
# configure UI cannot see -- but it is the right spot for a rule that should
# also cover other services, such as denying remote accounts.
